+  // LOCAL PATCH (2026-06-11): Chrome namespace-sandbox chroot support.
+  //
+  // FEX normally redirects guest absolute paths into the RootFS via a persistent
+  // RootFSFD (opened once at startup with fstatat(RootFSFD, relpath, ...)). That fd is
+  // immune to a later guest chroot(), so the guest's view of the filesystem doesn't
+  // actually shrink when it chroots. Chrome's NamespaceSandbox engages by chrooting to
+  // an empty dir (Credentials::ChrootToSafeEmptyDir) and then asserting
+  // CHECK(!HasFileSystemAccess()), where HasFileSystemAccess() == DirectoryExists("/proc").
+  // Because RootFSFD-relative resolution still finds <rootfs>/proc, the guest sees /proc
+  // as present, the CHECK fails, and the zygote dies silently before sending its HELLO
+  // message (browser then aborts at zygote_host_impl_linux.cc:221).
+  //
+  // Once the guest deliberately chroots, honor it: stop RootFS-redirecting absolute paths
+  // so they resolve against the guest's real (chrooted) root. A sandboxed Chrome process
+  // does not open new RootFS files after engaging the sandbox, so this is safe.
+  //
+  // Detecting the chroot reliably across FEX's process model is subtle. Chrome's
+  // ChrootToSafeEmptyDir clones a CLONE_FS|CLONE_VM|CLONE_VFORK helper that performs the
+  // actual chroot() and exits. FEX runs that helper as a separate process (it strips
+  // CLONE_VM), so a chroot performed by the helper sets state only in the helper, not in
+  // the parent zygote that subsequently runs CHECK(!HasFileSystemAccess()). Because the
+  // helper shares the fs_struct (CLONE_FS), the chroot DOES change the parent's real root
+  // too — only FEX's RootFSFD-relative redirection hides that from the guest.
+  //
+  // So this latch is detected from two directions:
+  //  1. A chroot() in THIS process sets it directly (NotifyGuestChroot).
+  //  2. Lazily, when resolving the exact path "/proc": if the real (non-redirected) "/proc"
+  //     has vanished, a CLONE_FS-shared helper must have chroot'd us, so latch it. This
+  //     covers the parent-zygote case where the chroot happened in the helper process.
+  mutable bool GuestHasChrootedFlag {false};
+  bool GuestHasChrooted() const {
+    return GuestHasChrootedFlag;
+  }
+
+  mutable int DbgFD {-1};
   int RootFSFD {AT_FDCWD};
   int ProcFD {0};
   int64_t RootFSFDInode = 0;
